NNAAMMEE
     ppffllooww — kernel interface for pflow data export

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppsseeuuddoo--ddeevviiccee ppffllooww

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppffllooww interface is a pseudo-device which exports ppffllooww accounting
     data from the kernel using udp(4) packets.  ppffllooww is compatible with net‐
     flow version 5 and IPFIX (10).  The data is extracted from the pf(4)
     state table.

     Multiple ppffllooww interfaces can be created at runtime using the iiffccoonnffiigg
     ppffllooww_N ccrreeaattee command.  Each interface must be configured with a flow re‐
     ceiver IP address and a flow receiver port number.

     Only states created by a rule marked with the _p_f_l_o_w keyword are exported
     by the ppffllooww interface.

     The ppffllooww interface will attempt to export multiple ppffllooww records in one
     UDP packet, but will not hold a record for longer than 30 seconds.  The
     packet size and thus the maximum number of flows is controlled by the mmttuu
     parameter of ifconfig(8).

     Each packet seen on this interface has one header and a variable number
     of flows.  The header indicates the version of the protocol, number of
     flows in the packet, a unique sequence number, system time, and an engine
     ID and type.  Header and flow structs are defined in <_n_e_t_/_i_f___p_f_l_o_w_._h>.

     There is a one-to-one correspondence between packets seen by bpf(4) on
     the ppffllooww interface and packets sent out to the flow receiver.  That is,
     a packet with 30 flows on ppffllooww means that the same 30 flows were sent
     out to the receiver.

     The ppffllooww source and destination addresses are controlled by ifconfig(8).
     fflloowwssrrcc is the sender IP address of the UDP packet which can be used to
     identify the source of the data on the ppffllooww collector.  fflloowwddsstt defines
     the collector IP address and the port.  The fflloowwddsstt IP address and port
     must be defined to enable the export of flows.

     For example, the following command sets 10.0.0.1 as the source and
     10.0.0.2:1234 as destination:

           # ifconfig pflow0 flowsrc 10.0.0.1 flowdst 10.0.0.2:1234

     The protocol is set to IPFIX with the following command:

           # ifconfig pflow0 pflowproto 10

